.TH CHAINBENCH 1
.SH NAME
chainbench \- time certificate chain verification, with controls
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B chainbench
[
.BI -n " reps"
]
.I host
.I cert.der ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Chainbench
reads the trust bundle, verifies a saved certificate chain against it
with
.IR x509 (2),
and prints how long each part took.  The certificates are DER, leaf
first, in the order a server sends them; openssl's
.B s_client -showcerts
produces them.
.PP
It needs no network and no server, so it can be run over and over on
the same chain, which is what makes it useful for working on the
speed: a handshake against a live server cannot be timed twice the same
way, and a server that loses patience hangs up in the middle of the
measurement.
.PP
After the timed runs it verifies four things that must fail: the same
chain against the wrong host name, the leaf with its issuers removed,
the chain with a byte changed in the leaf's signature, and the chain
with no trust roots at all.  A verifier that says yes to those is not
verifying, so
.I chainbench
exits with an error unless the chain verifies and all four fail.
.SH EXAMPLE
Save a chain and time it:
.IP
.EX
openssl s_client -connect example.com:443 -showcerts </dev/null |
	awk '/BEGIN CERT/,/END CERT/' >chain.pem
.I "(split chain.pem into DER certificates)"
chainbench -n 3 example.com c0.der c1.der c2.der
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/chainbench.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR p256test (1),
.IR x509 (2),
.IR tls12 (2)
